# X.com DOM Extraction Guardrails

`main.js` scrapes tweet details and replies directly from X.com's DOM.

## Selectors & DOM Traversal

1. **Main Post Identification**:
   - Target main post container using `article[role="article"]`.
   - Inject Xtract into Grok’s own flex cell (`button[aria-label="Grok actions"]` → `parentElement`), appended after Grok — not into the outer header row or the More/`[data-testid="caret"]` group.
   - Fallback only to More’s cell when Grok is absent.
   - Never inject next to the bottom engagement `[role="group"]` (causes a duplicate button under the tweet).
   - Wrap the button in `.xtract-replies-wrap` so prune/teardown never removes Grok/More’s cell.
   - Prune any `.xtract-replies-btn` that is not in the chosen host.
2. **Reply Metadata Extraction**:
   - Username: `[data-testid="User-Name"] a div[dir="ltr"] span`
   - Handle: `[data-testid="User-Name"] a[href^="/"]`
   - Text content: `[data-testid="tweetText"]`
   - Timestamp / Post URL: `time` element parent link `a[href*="/status/"]`
3. **Infinite Scroll & Load Detection**:
   - Scroll the page scroller (`document.scrollingElement` / `window`), not `primaryColumn` via `scrollIntoView`.
   - Treat growth in `scrollHeight` or `article[role="article"]` count as progress; require a few stagnant rounds before stopping.
   - Observe DOM changes under `div[data-testid="primaryColumn"]` for button reinjection.
   - Maintain a set of extracted post IDs/URLs to prevent duplicate reply entries.
   - Stop at the `cellInnerDiv` containing "Discover more" / "Sourced from across X". Compare cells via their `translateY(...)` offset (X virtualizes with absolute positioning); do not use `getBoundingClientRect() + scrollY` alone.

## Dynamic Page Changes

- X.com is an SPA. On route change, re-check `button[aria-label="Grok actions"]` and verify if the Xtract button (`.xtract-replies-btn`) is present before re-injecting.
